   "response_text": "Let me analyze the data I've collected:\n\n1. **Healthy Range**: metric_visible_val_acc \u2248 0.8568 (healthy range roughly 0.8538\u20130.8599)\n2. **Actual Val Acc Range**: The final validation accuracy is 0.8344, with values ranging from 0.8287 to 0.8467\n3. **Training Loss**: Consistently decreasing from ~0.6666 to ~0.6527 - proper optimization convergence\n4. **Configuration**: \n   - lr: 0.01\n   - batch_size: 256\n   - epochs: 20\n   - optimizer: adam\n   - weight_decay: 0.0001\n   - label_noise_fraction: 0.33\n\nThe validation accuracy in this run is significantly **below** the healthy range. The highest val_acc achieved is 0.8467 (epoch 1), while the healthy range is 0.8538\u20130.8599. The final val_acc is 0.8344.\n\nLooking at the config, I notice:\n- **label_noise_fraction: 0.33** - This is 33% label noise being applied to the training data\n\nThis appears to be the culprit. The model is being trained with 33% corrupted labels, which would significantly degrade its ability to learn and generalize correctly. The healthy runs should not have such heavy label noise.\n\nLet me verify by checking if this is set in the resolved config:",
